{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ftvncuqpj6pm3q4zid5q2syxoyztai7ugbxejfljcdsgvk7kbtza","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## Scope enumeration (done first, as required)\n\nThe library in `c_src/` turned out to be genuinely small — I confirmed this rather than assuming it:\n\n- `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` — builds a SHARED lib from exactly one source: `src/lib.c`\n- `c_src/include/lib.h` (14 lines) — the only public header\n- `c_src/src/lib.c` (21 lines) — the only source file\n\nI built the C library with CMake and ran `nm -D` on the result. The **complete** public ABI is a single symbol:\n\n```\nmd5_digest\n```\n\nI also scanned the header for namespace/renaming macros (e.g. `#define foo NAMESPACE(foo)`) — there are none, so the final linker symbol is plain `md5_digest`, not a prefixed variant. That one-symbol list was my definition of done. Given the size, delegating to sub-agents would have cost more than doing it directly, so I translated it myself.\n\n## What I wrote\n\n**`Cargo.toml`** — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` under `[lib]`.\n\n**`src/lib.rs`** — mirrors the C exactly:\n- `tflac_u8` / `tflac_u32` typedefs and a `#[repr(C)]` `tflac_md5` struct (verified 16 bytes, align 4, matching the four packed `uint32_t`s).\n- `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn md5_digest(m: *const tflac_md5, out: *mut tflac_u8)` — preserving the C signature (`tflac_u8 out[16]` decays to a pointer).\n- The 16 little-endian truncating stores happen in the **same order** as the C, inside a safe inner function (`md5_digest_impl`) taking `&tflac_md5` and `&mut [u8; 16]`; only the pointer reborrow at the FFI boundary is `unsafe`.\n- **No bug \"fixes\":** the C dereferences both pointers with no NULL checks, so the Rust does the same instead of adding validation that would change behavior.\n\n## Verification (my own commands, not self-reports)\n\n**Completion gate — symbol diff is empty:**\n```\n=== C exports MISSING from Rust ===\n                      <- nothing\n```\nBoth `.so` files export exactly `md5_digest`.\n\n**Byte-identical output** — I wrote a C harness that `dlopen`s the C `.so` and the Rust `.so` side by side, calls both through the same function pointer type, and `memcmp`s the 16-byte outputs. It pre-fills the two buffers with *different* sentinels (`0xAA` vs `0x55`) so any unwritten byte shows up as a mismatch:\n\n```\ncases=500005 mismatches=0 -> BYTE-IDENTICAL\n```\n\nThat covers 5 hand-picked edge cases (all-zero, all-`0xFF`, single-bit-per-byte-lane, sign-bit boundaries) plus 500,000 randomized inputs. Three unit tests on truncation/endianness/layout also pass.\n\n`cargo build --release` compiles clean with no warnings, and nothing in `c_src/` was modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":591,"cache_read_input_tokens":21075,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":591,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90e2aeb4-28d1-40a1-90c2-7e89c9250998","uuid":"ad41210b-418f-48df-8259-7a3f796f00a1","timestamp":"2026-08-19T10:24:13.668Z"}
